WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.986
Most vector databases spend their disk on one
thing: stored embeddings,

00:00:04.986 --> 00:00:06.716
one for every chunk of text.

00:00:07.037 --> 00:00:08.574
LEANN keeps almost none of them.

00:00:08.894 --> 00:00:14.357
And the paper behind it won a Best Research
Paper award at MLSys twenty twenty-six.

00:00:15.207 --> 00:00:17.065
You split your documents into chunks.

00:00:17.385 --> 00:00:19.500
You turn each chunk into an embedding,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.228
a long list of numbers that captures its
meaning.

00:00:22.548 --> 00:00:26.553
Then you store every one of those lists, plus
a graph that helps you search them.

00:00:26.873 --> 00:00:29.296
That pile of embeddings is where the disk
goes.

00:00:30.146 --> 00:00:33.113
LEANN comes from a Berkeley-led team of
thirteen authors.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.293
Their question: what if you do not keep the
vectors at all?

00:00:36.613 --> 00:00:38.113
Step one: graph only.

00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:42.670
It builds a normal proximity graph, a map of
which chunks sit near which,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:44.759
and then throws the embeddings away.

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:47.239
Step two: recompute at search time.

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:49.208
As a search walks the graph,

00:00:49.208 --> 00:00:53.331
it runs the embedding model only on the few
chunks it actually visits.

00:00:53.651 --> 00:00:55.382
Step three: prune the graph too.

00:00:55.702 --> 00:00:59.637
It keeps the links of well-connected hub
nodes, cuts most of the rest,

00:00:59.637 --> 00:01:02.111
and stores what is left in a compact format.

00:01:02.961 --> 00:01:04.331
So how small does it get?

00:01:04.651 --> 00:01:08.673
The paper reports indexes below five percent
of the raw data size,

00:01:08.673 --> 00:01:11.841
and up to fifty times smaller than a standard
index.

00:01:12.161 --> 00:01:13.911
The project's README goes further.

00:01:14.231 --> 00:01:17.361
It claims sixty million text chunks in six
gigabytes,

00:01:17.361 --> 00:01:19.192
instead of two hundred and one.

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:22.937
That example is the project's own measurement,
not the paper's.

00:01:23.787 --> 00:01:24.698
Now the catch.

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:27.047
Recomputing embeddings costs time.

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:31.559
In the paper's own tests, on one RTX forty
ninety graphics card,

00:01:31.559 --> 00:01:35.489
a single retrieval took between about one and
seven seconds,

00:01:35.489 --> 00:01:37.127
depending on the dataset.

00:01:37.447 --> 00:01:41.640
A plain in-memory index answers in about five
hundredths of a second.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:46.393
The authors say this adds less than twenty
percent to the total answer time,

00:01:46.393 --> 00:01:49.076
once the language model's own work is counted.

00:01:49.396 --> 00:01:54.719
But their own table shows twenty-three point
four percent on one dataset, HotpotQA.

00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:56.769
So measure it on your own workload.

00:01:57.619 --> 00:01:58.759
So who is it for?

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:04.156
Our take: LEANN is a smart trade if your index
lives on a laptop or a workstation.

00:02:04.476 --> 00:02:08.738
Think of a private search over your mail, your
notes, or a large codebase,

00:02:08.738 --> 00:02:13.173
where disk space matters and a wait of several
seconds per question does not.

00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:17.387
It is not a fit for a server answering
thousands of queries a second.

00:02:18.237 --> 00:02:23.283
LEANN is open source under the MIT licence,
and you can try it with three commands.

00:02:23.603 --> 00:02:26.194
The sources and the commands are in our
article.

00:02:26.514 --> 00:02:28.616
Read more at aitamer.news.
